# Proud history, part 2: more long reads. Same voice as pride.py.
# Each: id, era (colour), art (a scene in art.py), minutes, titles, summary, paragraphs EN and CY.

STORIES = [
 dict(id="longbow", era="medieval", art="fort", mins=5,
  en="The Welsh longbow", cy="Y bwa hir Cymreig",
  sumEn="The weapon that won Crécy and Agincourt was first the weapon of Gwent, and the Normans learned to fear it in the woods and passes of Wales.",
  sumCy="Arf Gwent oedd yr arf a enillodd Crécy ac Agincourt yn gyntaf, ac yng nghoedydd a bylchau Cymru y dysgodd y Normaniaid ei ofni.",
  pEn=["The longbow that made the armies of the Middle Ages tremble came out of Wales. Long before it stood in ranks on the fields of France, it was the weapon of the men of the south-east, cut from the wild elm of the hillsides and drawn by hunters and herdsmen who had used it since boyhood.",
   "## The bowmen of Gwent",
   "Gerald of Wales, who rode through the country in 1188, wrote that the men of Gwent were more skilled with the bow than any others in Wales. He described their bows: not of horn or yew, but of wild elm, rough and unpolished, yet stout and strong, made, he said, not to shoot a great distance but to deal deep wounds in close fighting.",
   "He gave examples that the Normans themselves had shown him. At the siege of Abergavenny in 1182, Welsh arrows went clean through an oak door a palm’s breadth thick, and the iron heads were left standing out on the inner side as a wonder. One of William de Braose’s knights was struck by an arrow that passed through his mailed thigh, through the saddle, and killed the horse beneath him.",
   "## A weapon of ambush",
   "That was how the Welsh used it. They did not stand in open fields to be ridden down. They let the mailed column come on into the wood or the narrow pass, and loosed at close range, where an arrow from an elm bow could go through mail, leg and saddle together. Then they were gone into the trees. Against that, heavy cavalry had no answer, and it is a large part of why the conquest of Wales took the Normans two hundred years.",
   "## Crug Mawr, 1136",
   "The year 1136 showed what it could do in a great battle. On New Year’s Day the men of Brycheiniog destroyed a Norman force in Gower. In the spring the princess Gwenllian led the army of Deheubarth at Cydweli and died at the head of it. And in October, at Crug Mawr outside Cardigan, Owain Gwynedd and his brother Cadwaladr, with Gruffudd ap Rhys of Deheubarth, met the whole Norman strength of the south-west.",
   "The Welsh bowmen broke the Norman line, the Welsh horse drove it in, and the Normans fled for the bridge over the Teifi, which gave way beneath them. The river was so choked, the chronicles say, that men crossed it on the bodies. Ceredigion was Welsh again, and Crug Mawr stands as one of the greatest victories ever won on Welsh soil.",
   "## From Wales to the world",
   "The kings of England learned the lesson. Edward I, who had faced the bow in Wales, filled his own armies with Welsh archers. At Crécy in 1346 Welsh bowmen and spearmen in green and white stood with the Black Prince, among the first soldiers in Europe to wear a uniform; the men of Llantrisant are remembered there to this day. Welsh archers were at Poitiers in 1356, and some five hundred of them marched to Agincourt in 1415.",
   "Owain Glyndŵr’s archers used it for Wales once more at Bryn Glas in 1402. For three centuries the longbow ruled the battlefields of Europe, and it began as an elm stave in the hands of a man of Gwent."],
  pCy=["O Gymru y daeth y bwa hir a wnaeth i fyddinoedd yr Oesoedd Canol grynu. Ymhell cyn iddo sefyll yn rhengoedd ar feysydd Ffrainc, arf gwŷr y de-ddwyrain ydoedd, wedi’i dorri o lwyfen wyllt y llethrau a’i dynnu gan helwyr a bugeiliaid a’i defnyddiai er pan oeddent yn fechgyn.",
   "## Saethwyr Gwent",
   "Ysgrifennodd Gerallt Gymro, a farchogodd drwy’r wlad yn 1188, fod gwŷr Gwent yn fwy medrus â’r bwa na neb arall yng Nghymru. Disgrifiodd eu bwâu: nid o gorn nac o ywen, ond o lwyfen wyllt, yn arw a heb eu caboli, ond yn gryf a chadarn, wedi’u gwneud, meddai, nid i saethu ymhell ond i glwyfo’n ddwfn mewn ymladd agos.",
   "Rhoddodd enghreifftiau a ddangoswyd iddo gan y Normaniaid eu hunain. Yng ngwarchae’r Fenni yn 1182 aeth saethau’r Cymry’n syth drwy ddrws derw cyn dewed â chledr llaw, a gadawyd y pennau haearn yn sefyll allan ar yr ochr fewn yn rhyfeddod. Trawyd un o farchogion William de Braose gan saeth a aeth drwy ei glun arfog, drwy’r cyfrwy, a lladd y march oddi tano.",
   "## Arf y rhagod",
   "Felly y defnyddiai’r Cymry ef. Ni safent mewn meysydd agored i gael eu sathru. Gadawent i’r golofn arfog ddod ymlaen i’r coed neu’r bwlch cul, a gollwng o agos, lle gallai saeth o fwa llwyfen fynd drwy’r llurig, y goes a’r cyfrwy ynghyd. Yna diflannent i’r coed. Nid oedd gan farchogion trwm ateb i hynny, ac mae’n rhan fawr o’r rheswm pam y cymerodd concwest Cymru ddau gan mlynedd i’r Normaniaid.",
   "## Crug Mawr, 1136",
   "Dangosodd y flwyddyn 1136 beth a allai ei wneud mewn brwydr fawr. Ar Ddydd Calan dinistriodd gwŷr Brycheiniog lu Normanaidd yng Ngŵyr. Yn y gwanwyn arweiniodd y dywysoges Gwenllian fyddin Deheubarth yng Nghydweli a marw ar ei blaen. Ac ym mis Hydref, yng Nghrug Mawr y tu allan i Aberteifi, cyfarfu Owain Gwynedd a’i frawd Cadwaladr, gyda Gruffudd ap Rhys o Ddeheubarth, â holl nerth Normanaidd y de-orllewin.",
   "Torrodd saethwyr y Cymry linell y Normaniaid, gyrrodd marchogion y Cymry hi’n ôl, a ffodd y Normaniaid am y bont dros afon Teifi, a ildiodd oddi tanynt. Roedd yr afon mor llawn, medd y croniclau, nes i ddynion ei chroesi ar y cyrff. Roedd Ceredigion yn Gymreig eto, a saif Crug Mawr yn un o’r buddugoliaethau mwyaf a enillwyd erioed ar dir Cymru.",
   "## O Gymru i’r byd",
   "Dysgodd brenhinoedd Lloegr y wers. Llanwodd Edward I, a oedd wedi wynebu’r bwa yng Nghymru, ei fyddinoedd ei hun â saethwyr o Gymru. Yn Crécy yn 1346 safai saethwyr a gwaywffonwyr Cymreig mewn gwyrdd a gwyn gyda’r Tywysog Du, ymhlith y milwyr cyntaf yn Ewrop i wisgo lifrai; cofir gwŷr Llantrisant yno hyd heddiw. Roedd saethwyr o Gymru yn Poitiers yn 1356, a gorymdeithiodd tua phum cant ohonynt i Agincourt yn 1415.",
   "Defnyddiodd saethwyr Owain Glyndŵr ef dros Gymru unwaith eto ym Mryn Glas yn 1402. Am dair canrif y bwa hir a reolai feysydd brwydr Ewrop, a dechreuodd fel ffon lwyfen yn nwylo gŵr o Went."]),

 dict(id="learning", era="saints", art="dolmen", mins=6,
  en="The oldest schools in Britain", cy="Ysgolion hynaf Prydain",
  sumEn="Caesar wrote that the learning of the Druids began in Britain and that students crossed the sea to master it. After Rome, Llanilltud Fawr became the oldest centre of learning in Britain.",
  sumCy="Ysgrifennodd Cesar mai ym Mhrydain y dechreuodd dysg y Derwyddon a bod myfyrwyr yn croesi’r môr i’w meistroli. Wedi Rhufain, daeth Llanilltud Fawr yn ganolfan ddysg hynaf Prydain.",
  pEn=["Wales was a land of learning before it was a land of castles. For two thousand years, in one form after another, people have crossed land and sea to study here.",
   "## The schools of the Druids",
   "Our first witness is Julius Caesar. Writing of the Druids in his Gallic War, he says that their teaching is believed to have been found in Britain and carried from there into Gaul, ‘and today those who wish to know it more thoroughly generally travel there to learn it’. Britain, in other words, was the home of Druid learning, and students came over the sea to its schools.",
   "Caesar tells us what they studied. Some stayed twenty years. They learned a great number of verses by heart, for the Druids held it wrong to commit their teaching to writing, though they used letters for other business. They were taught about the stars and their movements, the size of the world and of the earth, the nature of things, and that the soul does not die. The Druids were judges as well as teachers; they settled disputes between peoples, and they were free of war service and of tax.",
   "The Roman writer Tacitus shows where the heart of it lay. When the legions wanted to break the spirit of Britain in AD 60 they marched on Môn, Anglesey, the island of the Druids and their sacred groves. The treasures cast into the lake at Llyn Cerrig Bach, swords, chariot fittings and fine bronze brought from all over Britain and Ireland, show how far the island’s fame had spread.",
   "## Llanilltud Fawr",
   "When Rome withdrew, learning in Britain did not fail. It flowered in Wales. About the year 500, at Llanilltud Fawr in the Vale of Glamorgan, Illtud founded a school that is called the oldest centre of learning in Britain. Tradition gives it a still older beginning as Côr Tewdws, the college of Theodosius, founded in Roman times.",
   "The Life of Samson, written within a few generations, calls Illtud ‘the most learned of all the Britons in the knowledge of scripture, both the Old Testament and the New, and in every branch of philosophy, poetry and rhetoric, grammar and arithmetic’. His pupils are a roll of honour: Samson, who became bishop of Dol in Brittany; Paul Aurelian, founder of Saint-Pol-de-Léon; Gildas, the first historian of the Britons; and, tradition says, Dewi Sant himself. Students came from Brittany, Cornwall and Ireland, and tradition speaks of more than two thousand of them. The great carved stones of the kings and abbots still stand in the church there.",
   "## A land of schools",
   "Llanilltud was not alone. At Llancarfan, a few miles off, Cadog kept a school to which the Irish came; Finnian of Clonard, teacher of the saints of Ireland, studied there. At Bangor Is-coed on the Dee the historian Bede counted seven companies of monks, each of three hundred or more, all living by the work of their own hands. At Tyddewi, Dewi founded the house that became the mother church of Wales, and at Llanbadarn Fawr the family of Sulien kept learning alive into the eleventh century.",
   "When Alfred of Wessex wanted to restore learning in England after the Viking wars, he sent to Wales for it. Asser, a monk of St Davids, went to his court, taught the king to read Latin, and wrote his life.",
   "## The thread unbroken",
   "The idea never died. In 1406 Owain Glyndŵr wrote to the King of France that a free Wales should have two universities, one in the north and one in the south. In 1872 the University College of Wales opened at Aberystwyth, paid for in large part by the pennies of quarrymen, miners and farmers, who gave what they could so that their children might learn. Few nations have loved learning so much, or for so long."],
  pCy=["Gwlad dysg oedd Cymru cyn bod yn wlad cestyll. Ers dwy fil o flynyddoedd, mewn un ffurf ar ôl y llall, bu pobl yn croesi tir a môr i astudio yma.",
   "## Ysgolion y Derwyddon",
   "Iŵl Cesar yw ein tyst cyntaf. Wrth ysgrifennu am y Derwyddon yn ei Ryfel Galaidd, dywed y credir mai ym Mhrydain y cafwyd eu dysgeidiaeth a’i chludo oddi yno i Âl, ‘a heddiw mae’r rhai sy’n dymuno ei gwybod yn drylwyrach yn teithio yno, gan amlaf, i’w dysgu’. Prydain, mewn geiriau eraill, oedd cartref dysg y Derwyddon, a deuai myfyrwyr dros y môr i’w hysgolion.",
   "Dywed Cesar wrthym beth a astudient. Arhosai rhai am ugain mlynedd. Dysgent nifer mawr o benillion ar eu cof, gan fod y Derwyddon yn dal na ddylid rhoi eu dysgeidiaeth ar glawr, er eu bod yn defnyddio llythrennau at faterion eraill. Dysgid hwy am y sêr a’u symudiadau, maint y byd a’r ddaear, natur pethau, ac nad yw’r enaid yn marw. Roedd y Derwyddon yn farnwyr yn ogystal ag athrawon; torrent ddadleuon rhwng pobloedd, ac roeddent yn rhydd o wasanaeth rhyfel ac o dreth.",
   "Dengys yr awdur Rhufeinig Tacitus ble’r oedd y galon. Pan fynnai’r llengoedd dorri ysbryd Prydain yn 60 OC, ar Fôn y gorymdeithiasant, ynys y Derwyddon a’u llwyni cysegredig. Mae’r trysorau a daflwyd i’r llyn yn Llyn Cerrig Bach, cleddyfau, offer cerbydau rhyfel ac efydd cain o bob rhan o Brydain ac Iwerddon, yn dangos pa mor bell yr aeth enw’r ynys.",
   "## Llanilltud Fawr",
   "Pan giliodd Rhufain, ni phallodd dysg ym Mhrydain. Blodeuodd yng Nghymru. Tua’r flwyddyn 500, yn Llanilltud Fawr ym Mro Morgannwg, sefydlodd Illtud ysgol a elwir yn ganolfan ddysg hynaf Prydain. Rhydd traddodiad ddechreuad hŷn fyth iddi fel Côr Tewdws, coleg Theodosius, a sefydlwyd yn oes y Rhufeiniaid.",
   "Mae Buchedd Samson, a ysgrifennwyd o fewn ychydig genedlaethau, yn galw Illtud ‘y mwyaf dysgedig o’r holl Frythoniaid yng ngwybodaeth yr ysgrythur, yr Hen Destament a’r Newydd, ac ym mhob cangen o athroniaeth, barddoniaeth a rhethreg, gramadeg a rhifyddeg’. Mae ei ddisgyblion yn rhestr anrhydedd: Samson, a ddaeth yn esgob Dol yn Llydaw; Pawl Aurelian, sylfaenydd Saint-Pol-de-Léon; Gildas, hanesydd cyntaf y Brythoniaid; ac, yn ôl traddodiad, Dewi Sant ei hun. Deuai myfyrwyr o Lydaw, Cernyw ac Iwerddon, a sonia traddodiad am fwy na dwy fil ohonynt. Mae meini cerfiedig mawr y brenhinoedd a’r abadau’n dal i sefyll yn yr eglwys yno.",
   "## Gwlad o ysgolion",
   "Nid oedd Llanilltud ar ei phen ei hun. Yn Llancarfan, ychydig filltiroedd i ffwrdd, cadwai Cadog ysgol y deuai’r Gwyddelod iddi; yno yr astudiodd Finnian o Clonard, athro saint Iwerddon. Ym Mangor Is-coed ar lan afon Dyfrdwy cyfrifodd yr hanesydd Beda saith cwmni o fynaich, pob un yn dri chant neu fwy, oll yn byw ar lafur eu dwylo eu hunain. Yn Nhyddewi sefydlodd Dewi’r tŷ a ddaeth yn fam eglwys Cymru, ac yn Llanbadarn Fawr cadwodd teulu Sulien ddysg yn fyw hyd yr unfed ganrif ar ddeg.",
   "Pan fynnai Alffred o Wessex adfer dysg yn Lloegr wedi rhyfeloedd y Llychlynwyr, i Gymru yr anfonodd amdani. Aeth Asser, mynach o Dyddewi, i’w lys, dysgu’r brenin i ddarllen Lladin, ac ysgrifennu ei fuchedd.",
   "## Yr edau ddi-dor",
   "Ni fu farw’r syniad erioed. Yn 1406 ysgrifennodd Owain Glyndŵr at Frenin Ffrainc y dylai Cymru rydd gael dwy brifysgol, un yn y gogledd ac un yn y de. Yn 1872 agorodd Coleg Prifysgol Cymru yn Aberystwyth, wedi’i dalu amdano i raddau helaeth â cheiniogau chwarelwyr, glowyr a ffermwyr, a roddodd yr hyn a allent er mwyn i’w plant gael dysg. Prin yw’r cenhedloedd a garodd ddysg gymaint, nac am gyhyd."]),

 dict(id="unconquered", era="kings", art="ship", mins=6,
  en="The land that would not be conquered", cy="Y wlad na ellid ei choncro",
  sumEn="The Vikings made a kingdom in England and never in Wales. The Normans took England in five years; Wales held them for more than two hundred.",
  sumCy="Gwnaeth y Llychlynwyr deyrnas yn Lloegr ond byth yng Nghymru. Cymerodd y Normaniaid Loegr mewn pum mlynedd; daliodd Cymru hwy am fwy na dau gan mlynedd.",
  pEn=["Twice in the Middle Ages a conquering people swept over the island of Britain. Twice England fell, and twice Wales stood.",
   "## The Vikings",
   "From the 790s the longships came down on every coast of these islands. In England they came to stay. The kingdoms of Northumbria, East Anglia and half of Mercia fell; a Viking kingdom ruled from York; half the country became the Danelaw, under Danish law; and in 1016 a Danish king, Cnut, took the crown of all England.",
   "Nothing of the kind happened in Wales. In 856 Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd, met the Danish fleet and killed its leader, Gorm, and the news was celebrated as far away as the court of the Frankish king. When a Viking host under Ingimund tried to settle on Anglesey in 902, it was thrown out. In 893 the Welsh joined in the siege of the great Danish army at Buttington on the Severn and helped to break it.",
   "The Northmen left their names on the sea-marks they steered by: Anglesey, Swansea, Skomer, Skokholm, the Great Orme. They left no kingdom, no Danelaw and no king. The Welsh kings fought them, traded with them and at times hired their ships, and the land stayed Welsh.",
   "## The Normans",
   "On 14 October 1066 William of Normandy won one battle at Hastings. By Christmas he was crowned king of England, and within five years the whole kingdom, one of the richest in Europe, was his. Its nobility was swept away and its language driven out of court and law for three hundred years.",
   "The Normans reached Wales in 1067, when William fitzOsbern began his castle at Chepstow. They did not finish until 1283. For two hundred and sixteen years, against the most successful soldiers of the age, the Welsh fought for every valley. What the Normans took in one generation the Welsh took back in the next. In 1094 a rising swept them out of almost all the north and west. In 1136 they were broken at Crug Mawr. Henry II, lord of an empire from Scotland to the Pyrenees, led army after army into Wales; in 1157 he was nearly killed in the woods of Tegeingl, and in 1165 his greatest host of all was beaten back from the Berwyn mountains by the princes of all Wales standing together under Owain Gwynedd.",
   "The Lord Rhys made Deheubarth a power again. Llywelyn Fawr ruled almost the whole country, and in 1267 the king of England recognised his grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, as Prince of Wales. Edward I needed the largest armies and the costliest castles in Europe to bring it to an end, and he came close to emptying his treasury to do it. Conwy, Caernarfon, Harlech and Beaumaris are the measure of how much he feared the people they were built to hold.",
   "## Still not finished",
   "Even then Wales rose again: in 1287, in 1294 under Madog ap Llywelyn, in 1316 under Llywelyn Bren, and in 1400 under Owain Glyndŵr, who freed almost the whole country. And in 1485 a Welshman, Henry Tudor, marched from Pembrokeshire under the red dragon and took the crown of England itself.",
   "In 1163, Gerald of Wales tells us, Henry II asked an old man at Pencader what he thought of the king’s army and of the future of his people. The old man answered: ‘This nation, O King, may now, as in former times, be harassed, and in great measure weakened by your power and that of others, but it can never be wholly subdued by the wrath of man, unless the wrath of God shall concur. Nor do I think that any other nation than this of Wales, or any other language, whatever may come to pass hereafter, shall on the day of judgement answer for this corner of the earth.’",
   "He was right. The nation is still here, and so is the language."],
  pCy=["Ddwywaith yn yr Oesoedd Canol ysgubodd pobl goncweriol dros Ynys Prydain. Ddwywaith syrthiodd Lloegr, a dwywaith safodd Cymru.",
   "## Y Llychlynwyr",
   "O’r 790au daeth y llongau hirion i lawr ar bob arfordir yn yr ynysoedd hyn. Yn Lloegr daethant i aros. Syrthiodd teyrnasoedd Northumbria, East Anglia a hanner Mersia; rheolai teyrnas Lychlynnaidd o Efrog; aeth hanner y wlad yn Daenelaw, dan gyfraith y Daniaid; ac yn 1016 cymerodd brenin o Ddaniad, Cnut, goron Lloegr gyfan.",
   "Ni ddigwyddodd dim o’r fath yng Nghymru. Yn 856 cyfarfu Rhodri Mawr, brenin Gwynedd, â llynges y Daniaid a lladd ei harweinydd, Gorm, a dathlwyd y newydd cyn belled â llys brenin y Ffranciaid. Pan geisiodd llu o Lychlynwyr dan Ingimund ymsefydlu ym Môn yn 902, fe’u bwriwyd allan. Yn 893 ymunodd y Cymry yn y gwarchae ar fyddin fawr y Daniaid yn Nhal-y-bont ar Hafren a helpu i’w thorri.",
   "Gadawodd y Gogleddwyr eu henwau ar y nodau môr y llywient wrthynt: Anglesey, Swansea, Skomer, Skokholm, y Great Orme. Ni adawsant na theyrnas, na Daenelaw, na brenin. Ymladdodd brenhinoedd Cymru â hwy, masnachu â hwy ac ar brydiau llogi eu llongau, ac arhosodd y wlad yn Gymreig.",
   "## Y Normaniaid",
   "Ar 14 Hydref 1066 enillodd Gwilym o Normandi un frwydr yn Hastings. Erbyn y Nadolig roedd wedi’i goroni’n frenin Lloegr, ac o fewn pum mlynedd roedd y deyrnas gyfan, un o’r cyfoethocaf yn Ewrop, yn eiddo iddo. Ysgubwyd ei huchelwyr ymaith a gyrrwyd ei hiaith o’r llys a’r gyfraith am dri chan mlynedd.",
   "Cyrhaeddodd y Normaniaid Gymru yn 1067, pan ddechreuodd William fitzOsbern ei gastell yng Nghas-gwent. Ni orffenasant tan 1283. Am ddau gant ac un ar bymtheg o flynyddoedd, yn erbyn milwyr mwyaf llwyddiannus yr oes, ymladdodd y Cymry am bob cwm. Yr hyn a gymerai’r Normaniaid mewn un genhedlaeth, fe’i cymerai’r Cymry’n ôl yn y nesaf. Yn 1094 ysgubodd gwrthryfel hwy o bron y cyfan o’r gogledd a’r gorllewin. Yn 1136 fe’u torrwyd yng Nghrug Mawr. Arweiniodd Harri II, arglwydd ymerodraeth o’r Alban i’r Pyreneau, fyddin ar ôl byddin i Gymru; yn 1157 bu bron iddo gael ei ladd yng nghoedydd Tegeingl, ac yn 1165 gyrrwyd ei lu mwyaf oll yn ôl o fynyddoedd y Berwyn gan dywysogion Cymru gyfan yn sefyll ynghyd dan Owain Gwynedd.",
   "Gwnaeth yr Arglwydd Rhys Ddeheubarth yn rym eto. Rheolai Llywelyn Fawr bron y wlad gyfan, ac yn 1267 cydnabu brenin Lloegr ei ŵyr, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, yn Dywysog Cymru. Bu’n rhaid i Edward I wrth fyddinoedd mwyaf a chestyll drutaf Ewrop i ddod â’r cyfan i ben, a bu bron iddo wagio ei drysorlys wrth wneud. Conwy, Caernarfon, Harlech a Biwmares yw mesur cymaint yr ofnai’r bobl y codwyd hwy i’w dal.",
   "## Heb orffen eto",
   "Hyd yn oed wedyn cododd Cymru eto: yn 1287, yn 1294 dan Madog ap Llywelyn, yn 1316 dan Llywelyn Bren, ac yn 1400 dan Owain Glyndŵr, a ryddhaodd bron y wlad gyfan. Ac yn 1485 gorymdeithiodd Cymro, Harri Tudur, o Sir Benfro dan y ddraig goch a chymryd coron Lloegr ei hun.",
   "Yn 1163, medd Gerallt Gymro, gofynnodd Harri II i hen ŵr ym Mhencader beth a feddyliai am fyddin y brenin ac am ddyfodol ei bobl. Atebodd yr hen ŵr: ‘Gellir gorthrymu’r genedl hon yn awr, O Frenin, megis gynt, a’i gwanhau i raddau helaeth gan dy nerth di ac eraill, ond ni ellir byth ei llwyr ddarostwng gan ddigofaint dyn, oni bydd digofaint Duw’n cyd-daro. Ac ni thybiaf y bydd unrhyw genedl arall na hon y Cymry, nac unrhyw iaith arall, beth bynnag a ddigwydd wedi hyn, yn ateb ar Ddydd y Farn dros y gongl hon o’r ddaear.’",
   "Roedd yn iawn. Mae’r genedl yma o hyd, a’r iaith hefyd."]),

 dict(id="firsts", era="industrial", art="terrace", mins=5,
  en="Wales did it first", cy="Cymru oedd y cyntaf",
  sumEn="The first steam locomotive, the first passenger railway, the equals sign, the symbol for pi and the first radio message across the sea. A small country with a long list.",
  sumCy="Y locomotif stêm cyntaf, y rheilffordd deithwyr gyntaf, yr hafalnod, y symbol am pi a’r neges radio gyntaf dros y môr. Gwlad fach â rhestr hir.",
  pEn=["For a country of three million people, Wales has an astonishing number of firsts to its name. Here are some that every Welsh person should know.",
   "## On rails",
   "On 21 February 1804, at the Penydarren ironworks in Merthyr Tydfil, Richard Trevithick’s engine pulled ten tons of iron and seventy men nearly ten miles down the tramroad to Abercynon. It was the first time in the world that a steam locomotive had hauled a load on rails, a quarter of a century before Stephenson’s Rocket. The railway age began in a Welsh valley.",
   "Three years later, on 25 March 1807, the first fare-paying passenger railway service in the world began to run along the shore of Swansea Bay, from Swansea to Mumbles.",
   "## In numbers",
   "Robert Recorde of Tenby gave the world the equals sign. In The Whetstone of Witte in 1557 he drew two parallel lines, ‘because no two things can be more equal’. He also brought the plus and minus signs into English.",
   "William Jones, a farmer’s son from Llanfihangel Tre’r Beirdd on Anglesey, was the first to use the Greek letter π for the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its diameter, in 1706. Every schoolchild on earth now uses a Welshman’s symbol.",
   "## In science",
   "Sir William Grove of Swansea built the first fuel cell in 1842, the technology behind the hydrogen vehicles of today. Alfred Russel Wallace, born at Llanbadoc near Usk in 1823, worked out the theory of evolution by natural selection on his own and published it jointly with Darwin. Isaac Roberts of Denbigh took the first photograph to show the spiral shape of the Andromeda galaxy, in 1888.",
   "On 13 May 1897 Guglielmo Marconi, helped by the Cardiff Post Office engineer George Kemp, sent the first wireless message ever to cross open sea, from Lavernock Point near Penarth to the island of Flat Holm. The message was ‘Are you ready?’",
   "In the twentieth century Edward ‘Taffy’ Bowen of Swansea led the making of the first radar that could be carried in an aircraft, and Donald Davies of Treorchy invented packet switching, the method by which all data crosses the internet. In 1974 Tom Parry Jones of Anglesey made the first electronic breathalyser.",
   "## In trade and industry",
   "In the nineteenth century Merthyr was the iron capital of the world and Swansea, ‘Copperopolis’, smelted most of the world’s copper. The slate of Gwynedd roofed the cities of four continents, and Cardiff became the greatest coal port on earth; it is said that the first cheque for a million pounds was signed at its Coal Exchange.",
   "Pryce Pryce-Jones of Newtown started the world’s first mail-order business in 1861, sending Welsh flannel by post and rail to customers who included Queen Victoria and Florence Nightingale. The spare wheel was invented in Llanelli in 1904. Spillers Records in Cardiff, founded in 1894, is the oldest record shop in the world.",
   "## In how we live",
   "A thousand years ago the laws of Hywel Dda gave women rights to property and to compensation that the rest of Europe did not match for centuries. Robert Owen of Newtown founded the co-operative movement and opened the first infant school in Britain. Aneurin Bevan of Tredegar created the National Health Service in 1948, on the model of the medical aid society the miners and steelworkers of his own town had built for themselves.",
   "At Cardiff in 1905, before Wales played New Zealand, the crowd answered the haka by singing Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau. It was the first time a national anthem was sung before an international sporting match. Wales won.",
   "The firsts have not stopped. In 2008 Wales became the world’s first Fair Trade Nation, and in 2015 the first country to put the well-being of future generations into law."],
  pCy=["I wlad o dair miliwn o bobl, mae gan Gymru nifer rhyfeddol o bethau a wnaed yma gyntaf. Dyma rai y dylai pob Cymro a Chymraes eu gwybod.",
   "## Ar gledrau",
   "Ar 21 Chwefror 1804, yng ngwaith haearn Penydarren ym Merthyr Tudful, tynnodd injan Richard Trevithick ddeg tunnell o haearn a saith deg o ddynion bron i ddeng milltir ar hyd y dramffordd i Abercynon. Dyna’r tro cyntaf yn y byd i locomotif stêm dynnu llwyth ar gledrau, chwarter canrif cyn Rocket Stephenson. Mewn cwm yng Nghymru y dechreuodd oes y rheilffyrdd.",
   "Dair blynedd yn ddiweddarach, ar 25 Mawrth 1807, dechreuodd y gwasanaeth rheilffordd cyntaf yn y byd i gludo teithwyr am dâl redeg ar hyd glan Bae Abertawe, o Abertawe i’r Mwmbwls.",
   "## Mewn rhifau",
   "Robert Recorde o Ddinbych-y-pysgod a roddodd yr hafalnod i’r byd. Yn The Whetstone of Witte yn 1557 tynnodd ddwy linell gyfochrog, ‘am na all dau beth fod yn fwy cyfartal’. Ef hefyd a ddaeth â’r arwyddion plws a minws i’r Saesneg.",
   "William Jones, mab fferm o Lanfihangel Tre’r Beirdd ym Môn, oedd y cyntaf i ddefnyddio’r llythyren Roeg π am gymhareb cylchedd cylch i’w ddiamedr, yn 1706. Mae pob plentyn ysgol ar y ddaear bellach yn defnyddio symbol Cymro.",
   "## Mewn gwyddoniaeth",
   "Adeiladodd Syr William Grove o Abertawe y gell danwydd gyntaf yn 1842, y dechnoleg y tu ôl i gerbydau hydrogen heddiw. Gweithiodd Alfred Russel Wallace, a aned yn Llanbadog ger Brynbuga yn 1823, ddamcaniaeth esblygiad drwy ddethol naturiol ar ei ben ei hun a’i chyhoeddi ar y cyd â Darwin. Tynnodd Isaac Roberts o Ddinbych y ffotograff cyntaf i ddangos ffurf droellog galaeth Andromeda, yn 1888.",
   "Ar 13 Mai 1897 anfonodd Guglielmo Marconi, gyda chymorth George Kemp, peiriannydd Swyddfa’r Post yng Nghaerdydd, y neges ddi-wifr gyntaf erioed i groesi môr agored, o Drwyn Larnog ger Penarth i Ynys Echni. Y neges oedd ‘Are you ready?’",
   "Yn yr ugeinfed ganrif arweiniodd Edward ‘Taffy’ Bowen o Abertawe’r gwaith o wneud y radar cyntaf y gellid ei gario mewn awyren, a dyfeisiodd Donald Davies o Dreorci switsio pecynnau, y dull y mae pob data’n croesi’r rhyngrwyd drwyddo. Yn 1974 gwnaeth Tom Parry Jones o Fôn yr anadliedydd electronig cyntaf.",
   "## Mewn masnach a diwydiant",
   "Yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg Merthyr oedd prifddinas haearn y byd, ac Abertawe, ‘Copperopolis’, a fwyndoddai’r rhan fwyaf o gopr y byd. Toodd llechi Gwynedd ddinasoedd pedwar cyfandir, a daeth Caerdydd yn borthladd glo mwyaf y ddaear; dywedir mai yn ei Chyfnewidfa Lo yr arwyddwyd y siec gyntaf am filiwn o bunnoedd.",
   "Dechreuodd Pryce Pryce-Jones o’r Drenewydd fusnes archebu drwy’r post cyntaf y byd yn 1861, gan anfon gwlanen Gymreig drwy’r post a’r rheilffordd at gwsmeriaid a gynhwysai’r Frenhines Fictoria a Florence Nightingale. Yn Llanelli y dyfeisiwyd yr olwyn sbâr yn 1904. Spillers Records yng Nghaerdydd, a sefydlwyd yn 1894, yw’r siop recordiau hynaf yn y byd.",
   "## Yn y ffordd yr ydym yn byw",
   "Fil o flynyddoedd yn ôl rhoddodd cyfraith Hywel Dda hawliau i fenywod ar eiddo ac iawndal na chafwyd eu tebyg yng ngweddill Ewrop am ganrifoedd. Sefydlodd Robert Owen o’r Drenewydd y mudiad cydweithredol ac agor yr ysgol fabanod gyntaf ym Mhrydain. Creodd Aneurin Bevan o Dredegar y Gwasanaeth Iechyd Gwladol yn 1948, ar batrwm y gymdeithas cymorth meddygol yr oedd glowyr a gweithwyr dur ei dref ei hun wedi’i chodi drostynt eu hunain.",
   "Yng Nghaerdydd yn 1905, cyn i Gymru chwarae Seland Newydd, atebodd y dorf yr haka drwy ganu Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau. Dyna’r tro cyntaf i anthem genedlaethol gael ei chanu cyn gêm chwaraeon ryngwladol. Cymru a enillodd.",
   "Nid yw’r pethau cyntaf wedi peidio. Yn 2008 daeth Cymru’n Genedl Masnach Deg gyntaf y byd, ac yn 2015 y wlad gyntaf i roi llesiant cenedlaethau’r dyfodol mewn cyfraith."]),
]
